1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
The technology to prevent our own extinction by an asteroid.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:09,000
The power to wipe out our species with an engineered super disease.

3
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:16,000
The battle against cyber terror and the double-edged sword that is genetic engineering.

4
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:20,000
Now, more Doomsday Tech on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:41,760
Welcome to the year 2012.

6
00:00:45,040 --> 00:00:50,640
This insignificant space rock, a pockmarked orphan from the birth of our solar system,

7
00:00:50,880 --> 00:00:53,260
is about to exterminate the human species.

8
00:00:53,261 --> 00:00:59,720
It's a backdoor asteroid, masked by the light of the sun, and able to slip past the sparse

9
00:00:59,721 --> 00:01:02,580
telescopes in our southern hemisphere.

10
00:01:02,581 --> 00:01:09,120
Eight kilometers in diameter, it hurtles through space at 44,000 miles per hour.

11
00:01:09,121 --> 00:01:15,380
In a split second, the asteroid shoots through our atmosphere and makes a land impact.

12
00:01:15,381 --> 00:01:22,540
It releases the equivalent of 44 million megatons of TNT, 8,000 times the destructive power

13
00:01:22,541 --> 00:01:26,140
of the world's entire nuclear arsenal.

14
00:01:26,141 --> 00:01:29,780
It can really screw up the whole planet.

15
00:01:29,781 --> 00:01:34,980
First with a big explosion, then with lifting material up,

16
00:01:34,981 --> 00:01:36,640
blasting it off the earth, and it then

17
00:01:36,641 --> 00:01:39,380
falls back down into the atmosphere,

18
00:01:39,381 --> 00:01:41,160
where it bursts into incandescence,

19
00:01:41,161 --> 00:01:44,620
and the whole planet is enveloped in a firestorm

20
00:01:44,621 --> 00:01:47,720
with the forests and the grasslands burning.

21
00:01:47,721 --> 00:01:52,500
And then finally, by leaving much of this dusty material

22
00:01:52,501 --> 00:01:55,440
in the stratosphere where it blocks the sunlight

23
00:01:55,441 --> 00:01:59,480
and plunges the entire planet into darkness and cold

24
00:01:59,481 --> 00:02:01,360
that might last for years.

25
00:02:01,361 --> 00:02:04,880
It is this kind of environmental catastrophe

26
00:02:04,881 --> 00:02:07,780
that leads to a mass extinction.

27
00:02:07,781 --> 00:02:10,260
It wouldn't be the first time a successful species

28
00:02:10,261 --> 00:02:14,140
was destroyed by a rock from space.

29
00:02:14,141 --> 00:02:16,440
Most scientists now agree that the extinction

30
00:02:16,441 --> 00:02:20,220
of the dinosaurs and countless other species 65 million years

31
00:02:20,221 --> 00:02:25,380
ago was caused by an asteroid or comet up to 10 kilometers in

32
00:02:25,381 --> 00:02:29,460
diameter that struck off the Yucatan Peninsula.

33
00:02:29,461 --> 00:02:34,060
99% of the species that have lived on Earth are now extinct.

34
00:02:34,061 --> 00:02:37,820
And many of these went extinct at mass extinctions,

35
00:02:37,821 --> 00:02:41,880
at catastrophes in which the whole planet was affected.

36
00:02:41,881 --> 00:02:45,060
The evidence of violent impacts throughout our solar system

37
00:02:45,061 --> 00:02:48,880
reminds us that it's not if, but when.

38
00:02:48,881 --> 00:02:52,440
The Earth has been battered by impacts throughout its existence.

39
00:02:52,840 --> 00:02:56,080
If you want to get an idea how many impacts have happened on Earth,

40
00:02:56,320 --> 00:02:59,160
just look at our companion the Moon, which is covered with craters.

41
00:03:01,080 --> 00:03:04,860
By far, the most common cosmic warheads are asteroids.

42
00:03:05,580 --> 00:03:11,240
Well, an asteroid is a rocky body that's left over from the inner solar system formation process.

43
00:03:11,241 --> 00:03:16,880
And that material that didn't form into Mercury, Venus, Earth, and Mars,

44
00:03:16,881 --> 00:03:19,080
with the leftover asteroids.

45
00:03:19,680 --> 00:03:21,900
It's only within the last 20 years

46
00:03:21,901 --> 00:03:23,480
that astronomers have become aware

47
00:03:23,481 --> 00:03:25,980
of just how many doomsday-sized asteroids

48
00:03:25,981 --> 00:03:27,620
are swarming all around us.

49
00:03:28,440 --> 00:03:33,200
There's thought to be about 1,100 near-Earth asteroids,

50
00:03:33,320 --> 00:03:36,500
as we call them, that are one kilometer and larger.

51
00:03:37,160 --> 00:03:40,520
They come down from above, they come up from below.

52
00:03:41,180 --> 00:03:45,580
Their orbits are inclined to the plane of the Earth's orbit.

53
00:03:45,581 --> 00:03:49,720
The Earth is making its leisurely course around the sun,

54
00:03:49,860 --> 00:03:53,380
running through this cloud of near-Earth asteroids.

55
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:59,560
The odds of a planet threatening one-kilometer impact in this century are 6,000 to 1.

56
00:04:00,480 --> 00:04:06,400
But asteroids big enough to destroy a city come within 30,000 miles every few years.

57
00:04:07,300 --> 00:04:10,940
In either case, the potential consequences are so great

58
00:04:10,941 --> 00:04:14,200
that a handful of astronomers decided to do something about it.

59
00:04:14,201 --> 00:04:16,200
Asteroid hunting was born.

60
00:04:16,201 --> 00:04:19,200
Today's Space Guard survey, supported by NASA,

61
00:04:19,201 --> 00:04:22,200
is concentrating on finding the objects larger

62
00:04:22,201 --> 00:04:26,200
than one kilometer in diameter, because those are the ones that

63
00:04:26,201 --> 00:04:32,200
could risk a worldwide global catastrophe if they hit.

64
00:04:32,201 --> 00:04:35,200
Since the first serious asteroid hunts began,

65
00:04:35,201 --> 00:04:40,200
the crucial tools have been a telescope and a camera working together.

66
00:04:40,201 --> 00:04:43,200
The search is carried out with relatively small telescopes.

67
00:04:43,201 --> 00:04:48,060
telescopes but very sophisticated detectors and computers so that we can

68
00:04:48,061 --> 00:04:53,020
automatically detect the little faint moving image of an asteroid against the

69
00:04:53,021 --> 00:04:57,060
background of millions of fixed stars. With the electronic cameras that we have

70
00:04:57,061 --> 00:05:01,220
and the Quest camera that we are currently using, there's 112 cameras in one.

71
00:05:01,221 --> 00:05:07,520
We scan 20,000 images a night and completely analyze those in the night.

72
00:05:07,521 --> 00:05:12,180
The state-of-the-art Quest camera located at California's Palomar

73
00:05:12,181 --> 00:05:18,060
observatory is the world's largest digital camera. It uses CCDs or charge

74
00:05:18,061 --> 00:05:24,360
coupled devices. Each CCD contains millions of pixels, each of which

75
00:05:24,361 --> 00:05:29,340
converts the light it receives into electrons. Each pixels electron reading

76
00:05:29,341 --> 00:05:34,140
is given a precise digital value. Then all the digital information is reformed

77
00:05:34,141 --> 00:05:39,900
into an image. We report what we observe and then computers grind away at

78
00:05:39,901 --> 00:05:43,540
collating all the information that comes in from a large number of telescopes,

79
00:05:43,980 --> 00:05:47,080
and then plots the orbits of these things over a period of time,

80
00:05:47,280 --> 00:05:51,580
and then from those orbits you can infer whether it's going to strike the Earth or not.

81
00:05:52,100 --> 00:05:55,680
NASA's Space Guard program has tracked most of the large asteroids

82
00:05:55,681 --> 00:05:57,160
that could cross the Earth's path.

83
00:05:57,880 --> 00:06:01,840
And, so far, so good, nothing appears to be on a collision course.

84
00:06:02,580 --> 00:06:06,620
However, about 30% of our skies have yet to be surveyed for asteroids,

85
00:06:06,621 --> 00:06:12,060
mostly in the southern hemisphere, where there are far fewer observatories dedicated to the task.

86
00:06:13,260 --> 00:06:17,980
In 2002, an asteroid big enough to destroy an area the size of Ohio

87
00:06:17,981 --> 00:06:21,620
came out of the southern skies and passed extremely close to Earth.

88
00:06:23,020 --> 00:06:25,300
Only twice the distance is that to the moon.

89
00:06:26,140 --> 00:06:28,440
Astronomers had seen it coming only weeks earlier.

90
00:06:28,980 --> 00:06:34,740
The reason we're carrying out the Space Guard program is not that the probabilities are high.

91
00:06:34,741 --> 00:06:38,540
It's that there could be an asteroid headed for Earth.

92
00:06:38,541 --> 00:06:41,540
If it's going to hit Earth next year or next decade,

93
00:06:41,541 --> 00:06:44,340
it's already on a collision course.

94
00:06:44,341 --> 00:06:49,340
And we have the technology to find out and take action to protect our planet.

95
00:06:49,341 --> 00:06:53,340
So how do you stop a large asteroid from hitting the planet?

96
00:06:53,341 --> 00:06:57,340
Astronomers favor diverting the asteroid over destroying it.

97
00:06:57,341 --> 00:07:00,140
You can try and break it up Bruce Willis style,

98
00:07:00,141 --> 00:07:04,340
but then you've got this shotgun effect that's simply going to cause more damage.

99
00:07:04,341 --> 00:07:11,340
If you actually mount a shuttle engine, for example, on the surface of the asteroid,

100
00:07:11,341 --> 00:07:17,340
you'd want to thrust in this direction, so you move the asteroid a little bit in that direction.

101
00:07:17,341 --> 00:07:22,340
But the problem is, the asteroid's turning, so you'd have to thrust there,

102
00:07:22,341 --> 00:07:27,340
wait a while until it comes back around, thrust there, and so on.

103
00:07:27,341 --> 00:07:30,340
You can actually have a mass driver, they're called,

104
00:07:30,341 --> 00:07:32,900
anchor it onto the surface of the asteroid,

105
00:07:32,901 --> 00:07:36,580
electrostatically throw rocks from the asteroid off,

106
00:07:36,581 --> 00:07:41,580
and introduce a thrust that would slow it down or speed it up.

107
00:07:41,581 --> 00:07:45,080
A more viable current device called a solar sail

108
00:07:45,081 --> 00:07:48,700
would use the sun's energy to alter the asteroid's path.

109
00:07:48,701 --> 00:07:52,300
But it would still involve building a base on the asteroid's surface.

110
00:07:52,301 --> 00:07:54,100
Not an easy task.

111
00:07:54,101 --> 00:07:57,000
What you'd really like to do is get off the surface

112
00:07:57,001 --> 00:08:02,000
and introduce perhaps a solar concentrating mirror.

113
00:08:02,001 --> 00:08:06,000
So if you take the sunlight with your mirror and you reflect the rays

114
00:08:06,001 --> 00:08:09,000
and concentrate the rays onto the surface of the asteroid,

115
00:08:09,001 --> 00:08:12,000
you can actually ablate and vaporize the front surface

116
00:08:12,001 --> 00:08:16,000
and introduce a small outgassing in this direction

117
00:08:16,001 --> 00:08:18,000
and a thrust in this direction.

118
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:23,000
At NASA Langley's Comet and Asteroid Protection Program,

119
00:08:23,001 --> 00:08:27,000
scientists have modeled a moon base to track and divert a doomsday asteroid.

120
00:08:27,001 --> 00:08:34,000
A telescope on the moon could see the entire sky without atmospheric interference.

121
00:08:34,001 --> 00:08:41,000
And nestled, ironically, in a deep crater, it could avoid sunlight at all hours,

122
00:08:41,001 --> 00:08:44,000
allowing for constant observation.

123
00:08:44,001 --> 00:08:50,000
Once it had the asteroid or comet in its sights, the moon base would focus a laser on the target,

124
00:08:50,001 --> 00:08:52,700
on the target, generating enough heat

125
00:08:52,701 --> 00:08:54,180
to thrust the rock off course.

126
00:08:57,880 --> 00:09:02,060
Some of these technologies would be decades in developing.

127
00:09:02,061 --> 00:09:04,520
And lest we feel too confident, there's

128
00:09:04,521 --> 00:09:09,660
still the risk of the ultimate cosmic sucker punch, the comet.

129
00:09:09,661 --> 00:09:13,240
Comets, giant balls of ice and rock,

130
00:09:13,241 --> 00:09:15,620
have erratic orbits and emerge unannounced

131
00:09:15,621 --> 00:09:17,620
from deep in the solar system.

132
00:09:17,621 --> 00:09:21,660
If we should happen to be really unlucky and have a comet headed our way,

133
00:09:21,740 --> 00:09:26,320
we would probably have only a few months, at most a couple of years of warning.

134
00:09:26,940 --> 00:09:33,540
And in that time, I can't imagine that we would have any technological approach to protecting ourselves.

135
00:09:33,541 --> 00:09:37,340
The best we could do is just hunker down and let it hit.

136
00:09:37,900 --> 00:09:45,080
In 1994, human beings for the first time were able to observe the destructive power of a comet in our solar system.

137
00:09:45,081 --> 00:09:49,500
when fragments of the Shoemaker-Levy comet collided with Jupiter.

138
00:09:50,460 --> 00:09:53,760
The impact cloud was as large as the Earth.

139
00:09:54,120 --> 00:09:54,820
That's the bad news.

140
00:09:55,200 --> 00:09:58,420
The good news is there are so few of these comets

141
00:09:58,421 --> 00:10:01,600
that we really need to worry about the asteroids first

142
00:10:01,601 --> 00:10:03,520
and then deal with the comets,

143
00:10:03,521 --> 00:10:07,460
which are far more difficult to deal with later.

144
00:10:07,980 --> 00:10:10,400
With a little luck and our big brains,

145
00:10:10,980 --> 00:10:13,200
human beings may be able to avoid the fate

146
00:10:13,201 --> 00:10:15,700
that has befallen other mighty species.

147
00:10:15,701 --> 00:10:18,040
The reason that the dinosaurs checked out

148
00:10:18,041 --> 00:10:20,500
some 65 million years ago is because they didn't have

149
00:10:20,501 --> 00:10:22,540
a space program.

150
00:10:22,541 --> 00:10:25,200
Threats to our species can come from the far reaches

151
00:10:25,201 --> 00:10:31,700
of our solar system, or from microscopic weapons

152
00:10:31,701 --> 00:10:33,440
inside our bodies.

153
00:10:33,441 --> 00:10:36,540
The age of bioterrorism has arrived.

154
00:10:39,240 --> 00:10:41,240
The greatest mass extinction ever,

155
00:10:41,241 --> 00:10:48,000
The Permian Triassic, or Great Dying, wiped out nearly all life on Earth 250 million years ago.

156
00:10:48,780 --> 00:10:52,820
Recent evidence, while sparse, suggests a giant asteroid is the cause.

157
00:10:54,160 --> 00:10:57,240
More doomsday tech will return on Modern Marvels.

158
00:10:59,680 --> 00:11:05,900
In the weeks after September 11th, a stunned United States was rattled by another series of attacks.

159
00:11:05,901 --> 00:11:13,880
employing not large commercial aircraft, but microscopic spores of bacillus anthracis, anthrax.

160
00:11:14,580 --> 00:11:19,120
Five people died as authorities scrambled to find the source of the attack.

161
00:11:20,200 --> 00:11:23,289
Scientists from the Centers for Disease Control and the U.S.

162
00:11:23,290 --> 00:11:26,120
Army Medical Research Institute of Infectious Diseases,

163
00:11:26,880 --> 00:11:30,820
or USAMRID, immediately began analyzing the anthrax samples.

164
00:11:31,320 --> 00:11:35,660
While examining the spores from the letters sent to Senate Majority Leader Tom Daschle,

165
00:11:35,900 --> 00:11:37,900
They made a shocking discovery.

166
00:11:37,901 --> 00:11:44,720
I and my team looked at it by scanning electron microscopy just to get an idea of what its

167
00:11:44,721 --> 00:11:51,080
nature was, and we were amazed at how concentrated and how purified this material was.

168
00:11:51,081 --> 00:11:56,780
The observation was made very early that the powder seemed to be very energetic.

169
00:11:56,781 --> 00:12:01,100
It just sort of floated like smoke.

170
00:12:01,101 --> 00:12:04,680
This was exceptionally pure and well-engineered anthrax.

171
00:12:04,681 --> 00:12:07,680
The spores had apparently been treated with an electrostatic charge.

172
00:12:08,300 --> 00:12:11,440
So they repelled each other rather than clinging together,

173
00:12:11,900 --> 00:12:15,320
making them easily dispersed and inhaled.

174
00:12:16,140 --> 00:12:19,140
The two grams of bioagent found in the Senate building

175
00:12:19,141 --> 00:12:21,320
contained one trillion spores per gram.

176
00:12:22,320 --> 00:12:24,760
If it could somehow be perfectly dispersed,

177
00:12:25,220 --> 00:12:28,480
that's enough to provide 200 million lethal doses.

178
00:12:29,720 --> 00:12:31,940
Doomsday technology in an envelope.

179
00:12:31,941 --> 00:12:36,960
If a terrorist wanted to create mass casualties with anthrax powder,

180
00:12:37,700 --> 00:12:43,480
he would probably disseminate it as an aerosol plume, not delivering it in envelopes.

181
00:12:43,560 --> 00:12:48,340
It could be released in an open area through a crop duster or some such thing.

182
00:12:48,700 --> 00:12:55,480
If he had more limited quantities, it would probably be introduced into the HVAC of a closed air space like an office building.

183
00:12:55,660 --> 00:13:01,400
The anthrax attacks forced the world to acknowledge a new lethal combination of technology and technique.

184
00:13:01,940 --> 00:13:05,940
biological weapons, and terrorism.

185
00:13:05,941 --> 00:13:09,940
A bioweapons effectiveness can be measured in terms of lethality,

186
00:13:09,941 --> 00:13:13,940
ease in manufacturing, and infectivity.

187
00:13:13,941 --> 00:13:17,940
Anthrax, while lethal and relatively easy to manufacture,

188
00:13:17,941 --> 00:13:21,940
cannot be transmitted from one person to another.

189
00:13:21,941 --> 00:13:25,940
Other bioagents are only moderately infectious, but extremely lethal,

190
00:13:25,941 --> 00:13:29,940
like Ebola. Ebola is a viral hemorrhagic

191
00:13:29,941 --> 00:13:32,780
It is fatal in 50% to 90% of the cases,

192
00:13:32,880 --> 00:13:35,240
which makes it extremely lethal.

193
00:13:35,340 --> 00:13:39,180
But actually, it is not as easy to transmit Ebola from person

194
00:13:39,280 --> 00:13:43,080
to person as it is to transmit smallpox.

195
00:13:43,180 --> 00:13:47,160
Smallpox may be the true bioterror doomsday device.

196
00:13:47,260 --> 00:13:50,000
The dreaded scourge that killed over a billion human beings

197
00:13:50,100 --> 00:13:52,900
through the centuries could return as a weapon.

198
00:13:52,901 --> 00:13:53,601
as a weapon.

199
00:13:57,640 --> 00:13:59,740
The year 2014.

200
00:14:02,020 --> 00:14:05,020
10 suicide terrorists infect themselves

201
00:14:05,021 --> 00:14:09,020
with a highly virulent strain of virile major, smallpox.

202
00:14:09,580 --> 00:14:15,200
Walking through international airports, subways, and malls,

203
00:14:15,440 --> 00:14:20,320
they infect 20 cities in four continents within 48 hours.

204
00:14:20,321 --> 00:14:27,300
Ten days later, the first symptoms hit America, Europe, and the megacities in India and Brazil.

205
00:14:27,800 --> 00:14:32,000
Without enough vaccine to immunize the world, millions die.

206
00:14:32,520 --> 00:14:37,100
The smallpox virus, once eradicated worldwide, is back.

207
00:14:37,780 --> 00:14:44,480
Smallpox was targeted for global eradication by the World Health Organization in the 1970s.

208
00:14:45,160 --> 00:14:49,140
Global eradication was declared a success in 1980.

209
00:14:49,141 --> 00:14:54,220
At that point, naturally occurring disease had in fact been eradicated from the planet,

210
00:14:54,880 --> 00:15:01,700
and we would have been free of smallpox entirely had it not been for the Soviet weapons program,

211
00:15:01,860 --> 00:15:04,280
which developed smallpox as a biological weapon.

212
00:15:04,660 --> 00:15:08,920
Today, the smallpox virus exists officially only in two places,

213
00:15:09,580 --> 00:15:14,340
at the Centers for Disease Control in Atlanta and at the Vector Institute in Siberia.

214
00:15:14,341 --> 00:15:21,340
But that is a rosy picture, considering the vastness of the former Soviet bioweapons program.

215
00:15:21,341 --> 00:15:32,340
That program reached its full strength in the mid-1980s and continued until it was essentially shut down in 1990.

216
00:15:32,341 --> 00:15:38,340
The problem is that with literally ton quantities of smallpox virus being produced,

217
00:15:38,341 --> 00:15:42,340
it's unimaginable that all of that material was actually destroyed.

218
00:15:42,341 --> 00:15:47,200
The Soviets reproduced over a dozen lethal bioagents in ton quantity,

219
00:15:48,640 --> 00:15:52,560
including a genetically modified antibiotic-resistant strain of plague.

220
00:15:53,600 --> 00:15:56,200
But smallpox was the most massive project.

221
00:15:57,000 --> 00:15:59,820
A particularly virulent strain, called India 1,

222
00:16:00,300 --> 00:16:04,360
was cultivated inside a 300-gallon tank called a bioreactor.

223
00:16:05,380 --> 00:16:09,340
Inside, living monkey cells were maintained at the temperature of blood.

224
00:16:09,341 --> 00:16:16,920
Cell nutrient fluid and variolla virus were pumped into the bioreactor, creating a perfectly

225
00:16:16,921 --> 00:16:19,996
engineered smallpox breeding ground.

226
00:16:19,997 --> 00:16:24,440
One batch could produce 100 trillion lethal doses of

227
00:16:24,441 --> 00:16:28,930
smallpox. And they made thousands of batches.

228
00:16:28,931 --> 00:16:33,320
The Soviet bioweapons industry, while by far

229
00:16:33,321 --> 00:16:37,080
the world's largest, wasn't the only advanced biowarfare program.

230
00:16:37,081 --> 00:16:41,397
America did have an offensive biological weapons program.

231
00:16:41,398 --> 00:16:48,440
It was during the height of the Cold War, and it was discontinued by President Nixon in 1969.

232
00:16:49,400 --> 00:17:00,180
Between the 1940s and the late 1960s, the United States produced significant amounts of anthrax, plague, botulinum, and a host of other agents.

233
00:17:00,181 --> 00:17:07,320
Many were tested in the million-liter sphere, known as the 8-ball, at Fort Detrick, Maryland.

234
00:17:09,240 --> 00:17:15,120
Deadly agents were aerosolized, and spores and powders were packed into bombs and artillery shells.

235
00:17:16,040 --> 00:17:18,860
With these doomsday devices long ago dismantled,

236
00:17:19,580 --> 00:17:23,740
Fort Detrick today stands at the center of the battle against bioterrorism.

237
00:17:23,741 --> 00:17:30,740
Scientists here and at the CDC are doing their best to prepare for the worst.

238
00:17:30,741 --> 00:17:43,633
According to Russian scientists, the Soviet Union had developed an ICBM equipped with 10 warheads that could each release up to 4.5 pounds of smallpox mist over an

239
00:17:43,634 --> 00:17:44,740
American city.

240
00:17:44,741 --> 00:17:48,740
More doomsday tech will return on Modern Marvels.

241
00:17:48,741 --> 00:17:54,880
marvels. There were many strategies in the preventive war against a doomsday

242
00:17:54,881 --> 00:18:01,060
bioterror attack. The first is knowing your enemy, the disease. With highly

243
00:18:01,061 --> 00:18:06,140
contagious lethal diseases like smallpox, acquiring hands-on knowledge is a

244
00:18:06,141 --> 00:18:11,400
deadly serious task. Smallpox has to be studied under what's called BSL-4,

245
00:18:11,401 --> 00:18:15,560
biosafety level four conditions, the so-called spacesuit laboratories in which

246
00:18:15,561 --> 00:18:18,600
their highly secure laboratory scenarios,

247
00:18:18,601 --> 00:18:20,400
so nothing can escape from the laboratory.

248
00:18:23,400 --> 00:18:27,040
These researchers studying smallpox at the CDC,

249
00:18:27,041 --> 00:18:29,240
like anyone entering a level four lab,

250
00:18:29,241 --> 00:18:33,440
must first don the plastic biocontainment suit,

251
00:18:33,441 --> 00:18:34,920
often called the space suit.

252
00:18:37,320 --> 00:18:39,160
Every opening on the suit is taped shut.

253
00:18:39,161 --> 00:18:45,660
and the shell checked for leaks.

254
00:18:47,780 --> 00:18:50,000
Researchers are also tethered to an air supply.

255
00:18:54,340 --> 00:18:58,020
All of the air, both the intake and exhaust,

256
00:18:58,180 --> 00:19:02,260
goes through what we call high-efficiency particulate air filter, or a HEPA filter.

257
00:19:03,540 --> 00:19:06,440
The HEPA filter uses ultrafine fibers

258
00:19:06,441 --> 00:19:09,700
that can filter out particles down to one-third of one micron,

259
00:19:10,400 --> 00:19:12,000
the size of a small bacterium.

260
00:19:13,800 --> 00:19:16,760
Alarms sound if the filters experience a change in air pressure.

261
00:19:17,660 --> 00:19:20,740
Laboratory workers, when they leave the spacesuit area,

262
00:19:21,160 --> 00:19:25,840
go through a chemical disinfection shower before they exit the laboratory.

263
00:19:27,280 --> 00:19:31,900
Why do CDC scientists go to such trouble to study these last traces of smallpox?

264
00:19:32,180 --> 00:19:36,400
The smallpox virus is actually voted to be destroyed quite a few years ago

265
00:19:36,401 --> 00:19:38,560
by the World Health Organization, and unfortunately,

266
00:19:38,840 --> 00:19:40,740
bio-terror then raised its ugly head.

267
00:19:41,340 --> 00:19:43,660
And the decision was made to not destroy them

268
00:19:43,661 --> 00:19:44,640
so that it could be studied,

269
00:19:45,060 --> 00:19:47,120
so that you would not have something that then

270
00:19:47,121 --> 00:19:48,840
was in the hands of the bad people

271
00:19:48,841 --> 00:19:51,600
and not in the hands of the people that needed to be studying it.

272
00:19:53,080 --> 00:19:55,160
At USAMRID's Level 4 lab,

273
00:19:55,780 --> 00:19:57,760
Army scientists go through the same ordeal

274
00:19:57,761 --> 00:20:00,200
to study another deadly enemy, Ebola.

275
00:20:01,920 --> 00:20:05,200
We're interested in the basic pathophysiology of the disease.

276
00:20:05,201 --> 00:20:08,200
What is it about the infection with this disease that causes

277
00:20:08,300 --> 00:20:12,100
such a serious, life-threatening illness?

278
00:20:12,200 --> 00:20:15,240
We are also looking to develop antiviral drugs that

279
00:20:15,340 --> 00:20:19,540
are effective against the replication of this virus.

280
00:20:19,640 --> 00:20:22,160
The vacuum-sealed quiet of the research lab

281
00:20:22,260 --> 00:20:25,560
is at the heart of the effort to prepare life-saving vaccines

282
00:20:25,660 --> 00:20:29,260
and antibiotics.

283
00:20:29,360 --> 00:20:31,500
But if the unthinkable should occur,

284
00:20:31,600 --> 00:20:34,300
the response will be far from quiet.

285
00:20:34,301 --> 00:20:36,200
It will resemble an army mobilizing.

286
00:20:37,160 --> 00:20:38,880
If there were a bioterror attack,

287
00:20:38,980 --> 00:20:41,640
there would be a worldwide international response,

288
00:20:41,800 --> 00:20:43,640
despite where the attack happened.

289
00:20:44,480 --> 00:20:48,940
The first wave of response would be the CDC's Strategic National Stockpile.

290
00:20:49,580 --> 00:20:54,020
The Strategic National Stockpile is a program within the CDC

291
00:20:54,021 --> 00:21:00,060
that prepositions material at selected confidential sites across the United States.

292
00:21:00,061 --> 00:21:03,040
The stockpile itself is made up of medical supplies,

293
00:21:03,041 --> 00:21:08,620
Everything from bandages to hydration fluids to certain vaccines to antibiotics.

294
00:21:08,860 --> 00:21:12,480
They are in what are called push packs that can be gotten out the door

295
00:21:12,481 --> 00:21:15,180
into any place in the United States within 12 hours.

296
00:21:15,840 --> 00:21:20,220
Teams of epidemic intelligence officers, known as disease detectives,

297
00:21:20,780 --> 00:21:24,620
would fly to the affected areas and trace the path of the disease.

298
00:21:25,820 --> 00:21:27,840
Mass vaccinations would take place immediately.

299
00:21:27,841 --> 00:21:36,120
We would start out with vaccination in the area that the case occurred, and then you expand out from there.

300
00:21:36,800 --> 00:21:42,880
You have several days in which to vaccinate to either halt or ameliorate the effects of the agent.

301
00:21:43,800 --> 00:21:50,840
This same strategy, called ring vaccination, was used by the World Health Organization to eradicate smallpox in the 1970s.

302
00:21:50,841 --> 00:21:53,600
in the 1970s.

303
00:21:53,601 --> 00:21:56,540
Health care workers begin at the points of infection

304
00:21:56,541 --> 00:21:58,940
and increase in ever-widening circles

305
00:21:58,941 --> 00:22:03,260
until everyone who may have been infected is vaccinated.

306
00:22:03,261 --> 00:22:07,260
Coupled with this technique would be the use of quarantine.

307
00:22:07,261 --> 00:22:10,060
If somebody were to come down with smallpox infection,

308
00:22:10,061 --> 00:22:11,860
they would quarantine the person.

309
00:22:11,861 --> 00:22:14,700
And everybody that person had come in contact with,

310
00:22:14,701 --> 00:22:18,560
your house, your family, you know, everybody at work

311
00:22:18,561 --> 00:22:20,840
would be quarantined along with all of their family.

312
00:22:20,841 --> 00:22:23,000
there would be really extensive quarantine.

313
00:22:24,400 --> 00:22:30,040
The CDC has quarantine operations set up at eight major airports and several seaports.

314
00:22:30,780 --> 00:22:35,540
In the event of an attack, this will be the main weapon in containing the smallpox demon.

315
00:22:36,280 --> 00:22:40,640
Well, the vaccination uses what is called a bifurcated needle.

316
00:22:40,641 --> 00:22:48,860
It's a small dual-pronged needle in which it's pressed into the skin about 12 to 15 times on primary vaccination.

317
00:22:48,861 --> 00:22:53,500
and a virus replicates in that wound, causes a scab to form.

318
00:22:53,560 --> 00:22:56,560
When the scab falls off, you are immune from smallpox.

319
00:22:57,280 --> 00:23:01,200
The smallpox vaccine is, in fact, a cowpox virus.

320
00:23:02,180 --> 00:23:05,720
The word vaccine comes from the Latin word for cow, vaca.

321
00:23:06,400 --> 00:23:09,180
The man who discovered the vaccine, Edward Jenner,

322
00:23:09,940 --> 00:23:12,580
observed that cowhands who had contracted cowpox,

323
00:23:13,000 --> 00:23:17,100
a much milder form of pox, didn't contract the deadly human smallpox.

324
00:23:18,860 --> 00:23:25,060
In today's vaccine, the cowpox virus builds up a person's immune system to the point where it can fight off smallpox.

325
00:23:26,580 --> 00:23:31,020
Recent breakthroughs may also cut the doomsday potential of this and other deadly agents.

326
00:23:33,220 --> 00:23:39,080
Well, this is a handheld biosensor that we developed for the Department of Defense.

327
00:23:39,081 --> 00:23:43,800
Well, the system is very versatile and can detect a wide variety of different pathogens,

328
00:23:44,000 --> 00:23:47,860
including things like anthrax, smallpox, ricin, Ebola.

329
00:23:48,860 --> 00:23:53,880
A drop of the suspected pathogen is placed on this specially designed test strip,

330
00:23:54,360 --> 00:23:57,700
which is then inserted into the sensor.

331
00:23:58,560 --> 00:24:02,240
There's a small laser in here, similar to what is used in a CD player,

332
00:24:02,241 --> 00:24:05,080
that excites the species on the strip,

333
00:24:05,580 --> 00:24:10,160
and it measures the intensity of the light coming out as well as its position,

334
00:24:10,420 --> 00:24:14,440
and uses that to determine what species it has detected.

335
00:24:14,441 --> 00:24:21,520
It says that it has detected bacillus anthracis, which is anthrax, with 100% certainty.

336
00:24:22,980 --> 00:24:28,700
Such rapid results could trace the path of an outbreak in minutes, rather than hours or days.

337
00:24:31,820 --> 00:24:36,260
And while our high-speed interconnected world may hasten the spread of a disease,

338
00:24:37,980 --> 00:24:41,820
instant communication will also be crucial in containing a bioterror attack.

339
00:24:41,821 --> 00:24:48,480
whether it's the CDC's nerve center or health care professionals sharing their observations on the Internet.

340
00:24:49,760 --> 00:24:57,140
In 2003, an online medical forum called ProMed played a critical role in alerting world health officials

341
00:24:57,141 --> 00:25:01,400
to a deadly outbreak in China that would become known as SARS.

342
00:25:02,340 --> 00:25:06,280
China was very slow in releasing any information about SARS

343
00:25:06,281 --> 00:25:12,160
because they were worrying about the effect of its going into the World Trade Organization,

344
00:25:12,520 --> 00:25:15,640
about tourism, so it became hidden.

345
00:25:16,120 --> 00:25:20,200
So I went up on probe and asked, anybody else heard anything about this?

346
00:25:20,480 --> 00:25:23,880
And within 24 hours, it was acknowledged to have occurred.

347
00:25:25,040 --> 00:25:27,240
Although a naturally occurring virus,

348
00:25:28,000 --> 00:25:31,760
SARS stimulated a response that may mirror the defense against bioterror.

349
00:25:31,761 --> 00:25:39,660
within weeks the spread of the virus that killed over 900 people was contained the future of

350
00:25:39,661 --> 00:25:46,660
bioterror and the tools to fight it may be in the arena of biotechnology already scientists are

351
00:25:46,661 --> 00:25:52,160
unraveling the genetic secrets to the deadliest diseases but the technology may be a double-edged

352
00:25:52,161 --> 00:25:58,720
sword scientists recently used reverse genetic engineering to resurrect the single greatest

353
00:25:58,721 --> 00:26:05,920
plague of the 20th century, the Spanish flu that killed up to 50 million people in 1918 and 1919.

354
00:26:08,160 --> 00:26:11,360
Altering the DNA of a virus now makes it possible in theory

355
00:26:11,920 --> 00:26:15,840
to design a super virus that could wipe out large populations.

356
00:26:17,440 --> 00:26:23,360
In 2001, scientists in Australia inadvertently engineered a deadly strain of mousepox

357
00:26:24,000 --> 00:26:26,080
for which their lab mice had no immunity.

358
00:26:26,081 --> 00:26:34,240
The concern is that a similar trick could be used to introduce the gene into smallpox virus.

359
00:26:34,760 --> 00:26:38,640
And there's the theoretical concern that if such a strain were created,

360
00:26:39,120 --> 00:26:44,620
our strategy for containing the disease spread with vaccine alone might be flawed.

361
00:26:45,100 --> 00:26:47,740
If such a grim scenario became reality,

362
00:26:48,260 --> 00:26:53,400
we can at least take heart in the knowledge that human beings have defeated seemingly invincible plagues before.

363
00:26:56,080 --> 00:27:00,920
Hopefully, we can do it again.

364
00:27:00,921 --> 00:27:03,840
The word quarantine is from Quaranta Giorni,

365
00:27:03,841 --> 00:27:05,760
Italian for 40 days.

366
00:27:05,761 --> 00:27:07,960
It refers to the length of time that ships were forced

367
00:27:07,961 --> 00:27:10,680
to sit at anchor before being allowed to enter a port

368
00:27:10,681 --> 00:27:12,440
during the Black Death.

369
00:27:12,441 --> 00:27:16,440
More Doomsday Tech will return on Modern Marvels,

370
00:27:16,441 --> 00:27:17,960
here on the History Channel.

371
00:27:17,961 --> 00:27:31,140
Welcome to the year 2021.

372
00:27:31,141 --> 00:27:36,020
Genetically engineered wonder seeds lead to exploding crop yields nearly eliminating

373
00:27:36,021 --> 00:27:37,580
world hunger.

374
00:27:37,581 --> 00:27:39,720
But there's a problem.

375
00:27:39,721 --> 00:27:45,120
When the wheat seed was modified, a long dormant gene was activated, causing severe allergic

376
00:27:45,121 --> 00:27:51,200
reactions in nearly three billion people. Years pass before the connection is made.

377
00:27:51,800 --> 00:27:57,900
By then, the world's wheat supplies must be destroyed, and a global food shortage sets in.

378
00:27:59,740 --> 00:28:05,660
It's this type of doomsday scenario that has made genetic engineering, also called biotechnology,

379
00:28:05,661 --> 00:28:09,900
the source of angry protest and bitter scientific disputes.

380
00:28:09,901 --> 00:28:14,720
Critics accuse industry scientists of deceit in the name of profit.

381
00:28:15,680 --> 00:28:17,560
The critics are accused of fear-mongering.

382
00:28:20,000 --> 00:28:23,040
Genetically engineered products have been banned in some parts of the world.

383
00:28:23,680 --> 00:28:28,200
And yet, they're everywhere, from medicine to agriculture and beyond.

384
00:28:29,200 --> 00:28:34,540
Engineering with genetic material is already one of the most significant technologies of our young century.

385
00:28:34,541 --> 00:28:37,780
The 19th century was dominated by the age of physics.

386
00:28:37,781 --> 00:28:40,940
The 20th century was dominated by the age of chemistry.

387
00:28:41,360 --> 00:28:44,160
The 21st century is going to be dominated by the age of biology.

388
00:28:45,000 --> 00:28:47,020
So what is genetic engineering?

389
00:28:47,520 --> 00:28:52,380
Genetic engineering really is the use of what we call recombinant DNA,

390
00:28:52,700 --> 00:28:56,800
which simply means being able to cut DNA into very small pieces,

391
00:28:56,920 --> 00:28:59,440
cut out just a piece of genetic information,

392
00:28:59,900 --> 00:29:02,940
and move it from one organism to another.

393
00:29:02,941 --> 00:29:10,580
One of the most widespread and controversial applications of genetic engineering, or GE, is on food.

394
00:29:12,960 --> 00:29:18,460
In one sense, genetic modification of plants and animals extends far back into history,

395
00:29:19,160 --> 00:29:22,180
through the use of selective breeding and crossbreeding.

396
00:29:22,920 --> 00:29:28,160
But there's at least one big difference between classical plant breeding and recombinant DNA engineering.

397
00:29:28,161 --> 00:29:33,660
You know, in nature we can cross some biological boundaries.

398
00:29:33,661 --> 00:29:38,300
For example, we can take a horse and a donkey and we can cross them and we'll get a scarrow

399
00:29:38,301 --> 00:29:39,300
mule.

400
00:29:39,301 --> 00:29:43,540
But of course in classical breeding we can't cross a horse and an apple tree.

401
00:29:43,541 --> 00:29:47,720
The point here is in theory and practice we can now cross all the biological boundaries.

402
00:29:47,721 --> 00:29:53,080
So you can take genes from bacteria, viruses, animals, plants and humans and mix them.

403
00:29:53,081 --> 00:29:57,280
You can take a gene that creates antifreeze from a flounder and put it in a tomato so

404
00:29:57,281 --> 00:30:00,240
of the tomato will not react to frost.

405
00:30:00,241 --> 00:30:07,760
Here at UC Berkeley, my laboratory focuses on doing genetic engineering of cereal crops

406
00:30:07,761 --> 00:30:11,720
like wheat, barley, rice, corn, sorghum.

407
00:30:11,721 --> 00:30:17,040
We're involved in a developing country project with Africa trying to improve the nutritional

408
00:30:17,041 --> 00:30:18,040
quality of sorghum.

409
00:30:18,041 --> 00:30:23,520
There are other projects that we work on that are more directed toward perhaps consumers

410
00:30:23,521 --> 00:30:27,580
in the United States because we work on a project where we're trying to develop a hypoallergenic

411
00:30:27,581 --> 00:30:28,580
wheat.

412
00:30:28,581 --> 00:30:33,520
That is a wheat that people who otherwise are allergic to it and can't eat it would

413
00:30:33,521 --> 00:30:35,880
be able to eat.

414
00:30:35,881 --> 00:30:40,580
Creating the raw material for genetic insertion happens inside this small machine that has

415
00:30:40,581 --> 00:30:44,020
revolutionized biotechnology.

416
00:30:44,021 --> 00:30:48,860
It's a polymerase chain reaction, or PCR machine.

417
00:30:48,861 --> 00:30:56,080
Inside, enzymes called polymerases synthesize an unwound strand of DNA.

418
00:30:56,081 --> 00:31:01,300
In 45 minutes, it can generate millions of copies of a specific DNA sequence.

419
00:31:01,301 --> 00:31:07,320
A device called a gene gun then blasts the DNA from the foreign organism into the host

420
00:31:07,321 --> 00:31:09,440
organism.

421
00:31:09,441 --> 00:31:15,700
You use tiny little pellets, about 1 30th the size of an actual cell.

422
00:31:15,701 --> 00:31:19,880
You coat that pellet, which is actually made of gold, with DNA.

423
00:31:19,881 --> 00:31:25,580
You shoot it at a very high speed, about 600 miles per hour, down a chamber.

424
00:31:25,581 --> 00:31:29,120
And at the bottom of the chamber are plant cells.

425
00:31:29,121 --> 00:31:36,260
And those little tiny beads go inside of the plant cells and end up inside and become incorporated

426
00:31:36,261 --> 00:31:38,640
into the DNA of the plant cell.

427
00:31:38,641 --> 00:31:43,420
The gene gun also injects a genetic promoter, which makes sure the new gene is permanently

428
00:31:43,421 --> 00:31:45,240
switched on.

429
00:31:45,241 --> 00:31:48,440
And there you have it, a genetically modified organism.

430
00:31:48,441 --> 00:31:52,740
For critics, the promoter is just one of GE's problems.

431
00:31:52,741 --> 00:31:57,660
The promoter turns on the inserted gene permanently, but we now know that it can potentially turn

432
00:31:57,661 --> 00:32:02,620
on other genes inside the DNA permanently, which can create all sorts of things, allergies,

433
00:32:02,621 --> 00:32:06,180
toxins, new diseases, carcinogens, anti-nutrients, or things good.

434
00:32:06,181 --> 00:32:11,540
We don't know what'll happen if we change the DNA of our own organisms or of our gut

435
00:32:11,541 --> 00:32:13,740
bacteria.

436
00:32:13,741 --> 00:32:17,372
the unknown that causes critics of GE to urge caution.

437
00:32:17,373 --> 00:32:19,860
Unintended effects have been a common

438
00:32:19,861 --> 00:32:22,696
result of genetic engineering since it began.

439
00:32:22,697 --> 00:32:26,100
When genetically modified cotton was first introduced,

440
00:32:26,720 --> 00:32:29,365
many of the cotton balls just fell to the ground.

441
00:32:29,366 --> 00:32:31,740
Genetically modified soy cracked in the sun.

442
00:32:32,380 --> 00:32:35,720
These are examples that when you make changes at the fundamental level of the DNA,

443
00:32:36,140 --> 00:32:40,020
it's not like Legos. We don't just snap a gene into place and get one desired trait.

444
00:32:40,021 --> 00:32:46,840
In 2000, a brand of genetically engineered corn called Starlink, which had been approved

445
00:32:46,841 --> 00:32:52,120
for animal but not human consumption, became mixed with human corn supplies in grain silos.

446
00:32:54,040 --> 00:32:58,700
Starlink, which was engineered to produce its own pesticide, caused widespread allergic

447
00:32:58,701 --> 00:33:01,060
reactions, some severe.

448
00:33:02,500 --> 00:33:07,120
After the contaminated corn was destroyed, losses stood at a billion dollars.

449
00:33:07,121 --> 00:33:11,000
It was found in 22% of all samples by the USDA.

450
00:33:11,660 --> 00:33:15,460
And even after millions and millions of dollars and a billion dollar loss from Starlink,

451
00:33:15,900 --> 00:33:19,960
two years later it was still found in over 1% of the samples collected by the USDA.

452
00:33:21,020 --> 00:33:25,480
But perhaps the biggest surprise for genetic engineering came in June of 2000,

453
00:33:25,900 --> 00:33:30,560
when scientists from the Human Genome Project made the shocking announcement

454
00:33:30,561 --> 00:33:33,580
that our genome contained only 30,000 genes.

455
00:33:33,581 --> 00:33:41,740
up to that time the human genome was assumed to contain 100,000 genes each

456
00:33:41,741 --> 00:33:45,340
producing one of the hundred thousand proteins known to make up the human body

457
00:33:45,341 --> 00:33:52,660
but the numerical gap made it clear that many of our genes perform more than one function

458
00:33:52,661 --> 00:33:58,420
it was a wake-up call the foundation of genetic engineering is that they can introduce one gene

459
00:33:58,421 --> 00:34:02,980
into a species it'll create one protein it'll be the desired protein it'll act correctly

460
00:34:02,981 --> 00:34:09,980
and this foundation was destroyed when scientists acknowledge that a single gene can produce more than one protein.

461
00:34:09,981 --> 00:34:13,980
Critics say such a basic miscalculation of the human genome

462
00:34:13,981 --> 00:34:18,980
raises doubts about the precision with which any genes can be spliced,

463
00:34:18,981 --> 00:34:22,980
especially when dealing with the world's food supplies.

464
00:34:22,981 --> 00:34:27,980
Studies have shown the potential for unforeseen consequences on human health

465
00:34:27,981 --> 00:34:33,840
or on the crops themselves, which then infect non-GE crops with the spread of their pollen.

466
00:34:35,240 --> 00:34:39,080
